The root manifest exact-pins hyper = "=1.11.1" and patches crates.io to this
directory. The root lockfile changes only Hyper's version and local source;
the vendor lockfile is the unmodified released test/development lockfile.
The runtime enables only client,http1,server. Scoped upstream library tests
also need http2 for their shared harness; this adds no production protocol.

Raw-head cap invariant: an otherwise valid HTTP/1 head whose completion
requires more than 65,536 raw bytes must terminate at Hyper with HTTP 431 /
HeadersTooLarge before validated processor dispatch, policy rejection, or
protected upstream work. Malformed input can be rejected earlier by another
transport check; the size limit does not override that rejection. Raw bytes
include the request line,
leading empty lines, OWS, and the terminating CRLFCRLF; normalized headers
cannot recover that size. An exact-cap valid GET remains eligible for HTTP
200 and one upstream probe connection. The pre-service rejection audit hook
still runs and receives only the existing bounded transport classification.

The repair caps bytes exposed to each positive-budget read; it does not
promise an allocator-capacity bound. Hyper's existing parse loop rejects an
incomplete head at its maximum before another read. Body collection, header
count/deadline limits, processor admission, connection reaping (#286), and
successful/expired graceful drain (#274) retain their existing contracts.
There is no new parser, state, identity logic, or telemetry schema.

Ingress multiplicity invariant (#289): raw duplicate Content-Length fields must
remain visible to the typed policy even when numerically equal. The deterministic
regression fails before the role.rs repair (two zero-length fields become one)
and passes afterward. Actual production-listener/HTTP-recorder matrices live in
crates/eidolon-proxy/src/production_runtime_ingress_tests.rs. All 148 exchanges
passed in root's socket-enabled native workspace run. An independent public
listener / production-client probe also confirms the baseline forwarded equal
duplicate CL while this repair rejects it with no validated/upstream work.
The delegated sandbox's earlier bind EPERM was not protocol evidence. See the
exact-test inventory and limits in plans/2026-09-07-production-ingress-equivalence.md.

Partial-head terminal invariant (#292): malformed bytes already buffered behind
Hyper's partial-read fast path must receive the same parse rejection under
whole and fragmented delivery. The fail-first two-byte `".\n"` input produced
400/`MalformedTransport` as one read but an unaudited incomplete close when
fragmented. Independent review then found the same prefix padded to the 65,536
byte head limit produced 400 whole versus 431 fragmented. One full parse before
either terminal classification corrects both inconsistencies; it starts no
request or service work, adds no unbounded loop, and does not poll I/O again
after EOF. A syntactically valid smaller partial head remains
`IncompleteMessage`; a genuinely incomplete maximum-size head remains
`TooLarge`. Normal C4 driver regressions and the scoped Hyper connection
regression retain these outcomes.

Raw-fragment invariant (#292): a request-target containing a literal `#` must
not become accepted after URI normalization drops its fragment. Independent
review produced `GET /ok#fragment`; the exact pre-repair driver accepted `/ok`
under both schedules. The parser now returns its existing URI error before
conversion, so no complete request, policy, service, or upstream work begins.
The normal driver and server-parser regressions retain the 400 transport
rejection. These are parser behavior facts, not release qualification.

Raw-position HTTP/2 preface invariant (#292): prior-knowledge recognition must
not depend on whether leading invalid CR/LF bytes arrive with the remaining
input. A fail-first LF-prefixed preface was silent `VersionH2` when delivered
whole but a 400 parse error when fragmented. The parser error path no longer
strips leading lines before its exact-prefix check, so every prefixed form is a
malformed HTTP/1 request and only the exact position-zero preface receives the
special classification. This narrows special handling and begins no service or
upstream work.

